{"name":"Kingfisher Bright","hex":"#096872","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kingfisher","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various birds of the suborder Alcedines (or the family Alcedinidae sensu lato), having a large head, short tail and brilliant colouration; they feed mostly on fish.\ncommon kingfisher (Alcedo atthis)","common kingfisher (Alcedo atthis)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kingfisher"},{"term":"bright","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Emitting much light; visually dazzling; luminous, lucent, radiant.","Of light: brilliant, intense."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Brightness, glow.\n\n Glory, splendour.","Brightness, glow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bright"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kingfisher","blocks":[{"text":"Kingfishers are a family, the Alcedinidae, of small to medium-sized, brightly coloured birds in the order Coraciiformes. They have a cosmopolitan distribution: most species live in the tropical regions of Africa, Asia, and Oceania, but they can also be found in Europe and the Americas. They can be found in deep forests near calm ponds and small rivers. The family contains 118 species and is divided into three subfamilies and 19 genera. All kingfishers have large heads, long, sharp, pointed bills, short legs, and stubby tails. Most species have bright plumage with only small differences between the sexes. Most species are tropical in distribution, and a slight majority are found only in forests."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Alcedo_azurea_-_Julatten.jpg/330px-Alcedo_azurea_-_Julatten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingfisher","wikidata":"Q8154335"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Kingfisher_range.png/500px-Kingfisher_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_con